{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And on the Sabbath day.</b> It is found in Shocher Tov: among all the additional offerings you will not find an additional offering as small as that of the Sabbath. Because of this, the Sabbath complained before the Holy One, blessed be He. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to her: this is the additional offering fitting for you, for all your deeds are doubled — song doubled, as it is said: מזמור שיר ליום השבת; delight doubled, as it is said: וקראת לשבת עונג; its punishment doubled, as it is said: מחלליה מות יומת; bread doubled — לחם משנה. Therefore your offering is fitting to be doubled, and this is what we say in the additional prayer of the Sabbath: להקריב בה קרבן מוסף שבת כראוי — which is not said regarding all the other additional offerings. A parable: a king who said to his servants, 'Prepare a meal for the children.' They prepared for them two kinds of food. After they had eaten, the king commanded to prepare his meal. His servants said to him, 'What shall we prepare for you?' He said to them, 'What did you prepare for my children?' They said to him, 'Such and such.' He said to them, 'For me as well, do not prepare more than for my children.' So the Holy One, blessed be He, gave Israel לחם משנה on the day of the Sabbath; therefore He commanded to offer also לחם משנה, and this is: וביום השבת שני כבשים. And along the same line I will explain the meaning of תכנת שבת, and it is from the language of ותכן לבנים — meaning that you count day one, day two, etc., and the Sabbath falls on the seventh day, for the Holy One, blessed be He, loves the seventh. Therefore He took Enoch, who was the seventh generation, and likewise the Sabbath, and likewise the seven heavens, and likewise the sabbatical year, and likewise the Jubilee, and likewise the land of seven nations." "<b>Ratzit korbanoteha — its offerings.</b> The offerings that the Sabbath saw face to face, as you say: ומיום השמיני והלאה ירצה — and it is impossible to have eight days without a Sabbath." "<b>Tzivita pirushai im sederai niskeha — You commanded its explanations along with the order of its libations.</b> Tzivita — meaning the punishment of the Sabbath along with the command of libations, as Rashi explained regarding זכור ושמור — they were said in one utterance. And likewise לא יקח איש את אשת אחיו / יבמה יבא עליה — they were said in one utterance. And all of them appear to contradict one another, but זכור ושמור do not appear to contradict each other. One may say that it too resembles a contradiction, for from זכור ושמור we derive: זכרהו על היין בכניסתו — this is a positive commandment that is time-bound, and women are exempt; while שמור implies a negative commandment, for wherever השמר פן or אל is said, it is nothing but a negative commandment, and women are obligated. Thus it appears to come to contradict the inference from זכור. Nevertheless it is true that women are obligated in the sanctity of the day, for everyone who is obligated in guarding is obligated in remembering. And one may ask: why do we not read this passage of וביום השבת every Sabbath, just as we read on Rosh Chodesh and on the festivals their additional-offering passages? And one cannot say because this passage has only two verses, for we say in tractate Megillah that one does not begin with a passage of fewer than three verses — we could have read it and begun with the passage of צו את אהרן את קרבני לחמי, just as we begin even on Rosh Chodesh that falls on the Sabbath. One may explain that all reading of passages is for atonement, as we say in tractate Ta'anit: at the time our father Abraham said, במה אדע כי אירשנה — meaning, by what merit shall I inherit it? — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: קחה לי עגלה משולשת — meaning, in the merit of the offerings. And Abraham replied: this is fine when the Temple stands; when the Temple does not stand, what will happen to them? The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: they shall read the passages of the offerings before Me, and I account it for them as if they offered them before Me, and I atone for them. And in all the passages of the festivals and Rosh Chodesh, atonement is written in them, as it is said: ושעיר עזים אחד לכפר עליכם. But in the passage of the Sabbath no atonement is written; therefore we do not read it. Another explanation: because regarding the festivals it is written אלה מועדי ה' אשר תקראו אותם, and a verse does not depart from its plain meaning, which implies actual Torah reading; but regarding the Sabbath this is not written, and also it is not called מועד. And Rosh Chodesh is called מועד, as it is written: קרא עלי מועד וגומר. On Rosh Chodesh it is written לחטאת לה' — which is not said regarding all the additional offerings; and in tractate Shavuot it is derived: the Holy One, blessed be He, said: bring atonement for Me, for I diminished the moon. And one may explain: כן תביאו לדורות בשבילי ולשמי קרבן — to be an offering of atonement for Me, and thereby the moon will be comforted for My having diminished it. And this atonement benefits infants, so that croup does not fall upon them, because the first new moon was on the fourth day, as we say: on the fourth day the luminaries were hung, and Master said: on the fourth day they would fast against croup so it would not fall upon infants. And also Rashi explained in the passage of Genesis that therefore it is written יהי מארת — defective, a language of מארה (curse), and all the more so on the day of the moon's renewal there is reason to worry about infants, and this Rosh Chodesh offering atones for them. And this is what we say in the additional prayer of Rosh Chodesh: זמן כפרה לכל תולדותם — ותולדותיו של אדם — these are the infants."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The text alternates between third-person reference to God (הקדוש ברוך הוא / הקב\"ה) and direct speech; preserved as written. תכנת שבת is a phrase from the Sabbath musaf liturgy; the note reads it as related to ותכן לבנים in Isaiah 65:22.", 1 "The note hinges on the wordplay between the lemma (from the Sabbath musaf piyyut) and the verse in Ezekiel 43:27; preserved literally.", 2 "The lemma is a phrase from the Sabbath musaf piyyut; the comment ranges across liturgical, halakhic, and midrashic material. The phrase קרא עלי מועד is from Job 3:6. The closing explanation links תולדותם in the Rosh Chodesh musaf liturgy with תולדותיו של אדם as infants."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:40.125393160Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}